.\" SPDX-License-Identifier: BSD-3-Clause
.Dd September 25, 2026
.Dt RCORDER 8
.Os OxideBSD
.Sh NAME
.Nm rcorder
.Nd print a dependency ordering of interdependent files
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Fl gp
.Op Fl k Ar keep
.Op Fl s Ar skip
.Ar
.Sh DESCRIPTION
The
.Nm
utility reads the dependency declarations of the named files, which are
normally the scripts in
.Pa /etc/rc.d ,
and prints the files so that every file appears after all files it depends
on.
.Xr rc 8
uses it to decide the order in which services start.
.Pp
A file declares its dependencies in one of two ways.
Classic
.Xr rc.subr 8
scripts use comment lines at the top of the file:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
# PROVIDE: cron
# REQUIRE: LOGIN FILESYSTEMS
# BEFORE:  securelevel
# KEYWORD: shutdown
.Ed
.Pp
The lines must be contiguous; the first other line after them ends the
header.
Scripts written in the
.Xr init_sh 8
dialect instead declare a service block, whose
.Ic provide ,
.Ic require ,
.Ic before
and
.Ic keyword
fields have the same meanings:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
service cron {
	require LOGIN FILESYSTEMS
	keyword shutdown
}
.Ed
.Pp
A service block that has no
.Ic provide
field provides its own name.
The block is parsed but never run.
A set of files may mix both forms.
.Pp
.Dq PROVIDE
names the conditions a file provides,
.Dq REQUIRE
the conditions that must be provided first, and
.Dq BEFORE
conditions that must come after this file.
Several files may provide the same condition; a file requiring it comes
after all of them.
Files not otherwise constrained keep the order given on the command line.
.Pp
The options are as follows:
.Bl -tag -width indent
.It Fl g
Print the dependency graph in the
.Xr dot 1
language instead of a list.
Edges point in the order files run; edges removed to break a cycle, and
requirements nobody provides, are drawn in red.
.It Fl k Ar keep
Print only files that have the keyword
.Ar keep .
May be given more than once, to keep files having any of the keywords.
.It Fl p
Print files that may run at the same time on one line.
Each line depends only on lines before it.
.It Fl s Ar skip
Do not print files that have the keyword
.Ar skip .
May be given more than once.
.El
.Pp
Filtered files are still used to order the others.
.Sh EXIT STATUS
The
.Nm
utility exits 0 on success, and 1 if a file cannot be read or the
dependencies contain a cycle.
A cycle is broken where it is found and reported, and a complete order is
still printed, so the system can boot.
A requirement that no file provides is reported but is not an error.
.Sh EXAMPLES
Print the order in which services start, and the ones stopped at shutdown:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ rcorder /etc/rc.d/*
$ rcorder -k shutdown /etc/rc.d/*
.Ed
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr rc.conf 5 ,
.Xr init_sh 8 ,
.Xr rc 8 ,
.Xr rc.subr 8
.Sh HISTORY
An
.Nm
utility first appeared in
.Nx 1.5 .
This implementation was written for OxideBSD.
